00:24:38.706 --> 00:24:40.386
index is computed based on what

00:24:40.386 --> 00:24:42.066
hospitals actually pay their staff.

00:24:42.786 --> 00:24:44.466
When you run the raw data for

00:24:44.466 --> 00:24:46.466
Puerto Rico, their natural

00:24:46.466 --> 00:24:48.066
mathematically computed wage

00:24:48.066 --> 00:24:51.266
index comes out to roughly 0.35.

00:24:51.266 --> 00:24:54.066
0.35, that is the absolute lowest

00:24:54.066 --> 00:24:55.586
in the entire nation by a wide

00:24:55.586 --> 00:24:57.026
margin. And it is crucial to

00:24:57.026 --> 00:24:58.226
clarify here for you guys

00:24:58.226 --> 00:24:59.906
listening that this 0.35

00:24:59.906 --> 00:25:02.066
index is not because the work of a

00:25:02.066 --> 00:25:04.626
highly trained ICU nurse in San Juan

00:25:04.626 --> 00:25:06.146
is inherently worth a third of the

00:25:06.146 --> 00:25:07.266
work of a nurse in Florida.

00:25:07.266 --> 00:25:08.066
No, not at all.

00:25:08.066 --> 00:25:10.146
It is a mathematical artifact of a

00:25:10.146 --> 00:25:11.666
totally flawed formula.

00:25:11.666 --> 00:25:13.346
Hospitals can only afford to pay

00:25:13.346 --> 00:25:15.106
their staff based on the revenue

00:25:15.106 --> 00:25:15.746
they generate right.

00:25:15.746 --> 00:25:17.106
Right, basic economics.

00:25:17.106 --> 00:25:18.546
For many of these hospitals, their

00:25:18.546 --> 00:25:19.906
primary sorts of revenue is

00:25:19.906 --> 00:25:21.506
Medicare reimbursements.

00:25:21.506 --> 00:25:23.106
But Medicare reimburses them

00:25:23.106 --> 00:25:24.306
based on what they are currently

00:25:24.306 --> 00:25:25.426
paying their staff.

00:25:25.426 --> 00:25:26.946
It's the ultimate catch 22.

00:25:27.826 --> 00:25:30.226
Low Medicare reimbursement forces

00:25:30.226 --> 00:25:31.986
the hospital to pay low wages just

00:25:31.986 --> 00:25:33.426
to keep the lights on and the doors

00:25:33.426 --> 00:25:33.666
open.

00:25:33.666 --> 00:25:34.386
Yeah.

00:25:34.386 --> 00:25:35.586
Those low wages are then

00:25:35.586 --> 00:25:36.946
dutifully recorded on a cost

00:25:36.946 --> 00:25:38.306
report and sent to CMS.

00:25:38.306 --> 00:25:40.386
And then CMS plugs those low wages

00:25:40.386 --> 00:25:41.186
into the formula.

00:25:41.186 --> 00:25:43.746
Which generates an abysmal 0.35

00:25:43.746 --> 00:25:44.546
wage index.

00:25:45.186 --> 00:25:46.466
Which then dictates that the

00:25:46.466 --> 00:25:48.626
hospital must receive low

00:25:48.626 --> 00:25:49.826
Medicare reimbursement.

00:25:49.826 --> 00:25:51.746
The spreadsheet literally measures

00:25:51.746 --> 00:25:53.026
the poverty it created in the

00:25:53.026 --> 00:25:53.906
first place and then

00:25:53.906 --> 00:25:55.186
institutionalizes it.

00:25:55.186 --> 00:25:55.986
Recisely.

00:25:55.986 --> 00:25:57.586
It locks them into a perpetual

00:25:57.586 --> 00:25:59.266
cycle of underfunding.

00:25:59.266 --> 00:26:00.626
Now for a brief period from

00:26:00.626 --> 00:26:02.706
roughly 2020 to 2024,

00:26:02.706 --> 00:26:03.666
the federal government

00:26:03.666 --> 00:26:05.266
actually attempted to intervene.

00:26:05.266 --> 00:26:06.386
What did they do?

00:26:06.386 --> 00:26:07.906
Well, the Centers for Medicare

00:26:07.906 --> 00:26:09.906
and Medicaid Services or CMS

00:26:09.906 --> 00:26:11.266
recognized that this circularity

00:26:11.266 --> 00:26:12.626
trap was destroying hospitals

00:26:12.626 --> 00:26:14.146
nationwide, not just in Puerto

00:26:14.146 --> 00:26:15.026
Rico.

00:26:15.026 --> 00:26:16.066
So they implemented what they

00:26:16.066 --> 00:26:17.746
called a low wage index

00:26:17.746 --> 00:26:18.946
hospital policy.

00:26:18.946 --> 00:26:20.066
Okay, so effects.

00:26:20.066 --> 00:26:20.466
Kind of.

00:26:20.466 --> 00:26:21.346
This was essentially an

00:26:21.346 --> 00:26:22.946
administrative band aid

00:26:22.946 --> 00:26:24.226
that artificially boosted

00:26:24.226 --> 00:26:25.906
the wage index for the lowest

00:26:25.906 --> 00:26:27.346
quartile of hospitals across the

00:26:27.346 --> 00:26:27.906
country.

00:26:27.906 --> 00:26:29.426
So they just arbitrarily raised

00:26:29.426 --> 00:26:30.546
the floor for the poorest

00:26:30.546 --> 00:26:31.586
hospitals.

00:26:31.586 --> 00:26:33.826
Yes, they used administrative

00:26:33.826 --> 00:26:35.266
discretion to just inflate the

00:26:35.266 --> 00:26:36.946
numbers from the bottom up.

00:26:36.946 --> 00:26:39.026
For Puerto Rico, this temporary

00:26:39.026 --> 00:26:41.106
patch lifted their effective

00:26:41.106 --> 00:26:42.786
operational wage index

00:26:42.786 --> 00:26:45.026
from that natural 0.35

00:26:45.026 --> 00:26:46.626
up to around 0.61.

00:26:46.626 --> 00:26:48.066
That's a huge jump.

00:26:48.066 --> 00:26:48.946
It nearly doubled their

00:26:48.946 --> 00:26:49.826
protection overnight.

00:26:49.826 --> 00:26:51.506
It was a massive influx of

00:26:51.506 --> 00:26:53.186
desperately needed stabilization

00:26:53.186 --> 00:26:53.986
funding.

00:26:53.986 --> 00:26:55.426
But the problem with administrative

00:26:55.426 --> 00:26:56.946
patches is that they can be ripped

00:26:56.946 --> 00:26:57.506
off.

00:26:57.506 --> 00:26:58.066
Very true.

00:26:58.066 --> 00:26:59.586
And that is exactly what happened

00:26:59.586 --> 00:27:01.186
in July of 2024.

00:27:01.826 --> 00:27:03.906
The DC Circuit Court of Appeals

00:27:03.906 --> 00:27:06.146
issued a major ruling in a case

00:27:06.146 --> 00:27:07.746
called Bridgeport Hospital v.

00:27:07.746 --> 00:27:08.306
Becerra.

00:27:09.186 --> 00:27:10.706
What exactly was the legal

00:27:10.706 --> 00:27:11.506
argument there?

00:27:11.506 --> 00:27:12.866
Why did a court strike down a

00:27:12.866 --> 00:27:14.626
policy that was specifically meant

00:27:14.626 --> 00:27:15.986
to help poor hospitals?

00:27:15.986 --> 00:27:17.026
It all comes back to the

00:27:17.026 --> 00:27:19.106
tension between statutory text

00:27:19.106 --> 00:27:20.386
and administrative action,

00:27:20.386 --> 00:27:21.346
much like the Nantucket

00:27:21.346 --> 00:27:23.026
situation we talked about earlier.

00:27:23.026 --> 00:27:24.226
In the Bridgeport case,

00:27:24.226 --> 00:27:25.826
a group of higher paid hospitals

00:27:25.826 --> 00:27:27.346
basically sued the Secretary of

00:27:27.346 --> 00:27:28.386
Health and Human Services.

00:27:28.386 --> 00:27:29.666
Why were they mad?

00:27:29.666 --> 00:27:31.426
They argued that CMS did not have

00:27:31.426 --> 00:27:32.706
the statutory authority

00:27:32.706 --> 00:27:34.466
to artificially boost the wages

00:27:34.466 --> 00:27:35.666
of poor hospitals.

00:27:35.666 --> 00:27:36.946
Remember, budget neutrality.

00:27:36.946 --> 00:27:37.746
Oh, right.

00:27:37.746 --> 00:27:38.946
Because to boost the bottom,

00:27:38.946 --> 00:27:40.066
25 percent?

00:27:40.066 --> 00:27:41.506
CMS had to cut the payments

00:27:41.506 --> 00:27:43.026
for the top 75 percent to pay

00:27:43.026 --> 00:27:43.586
for it.

00:27:43.586 --> 00:27:44.146
Wow.

00:27:44.146 --> 00:27:45.826
So the rich hospitals sued to

00:27:45.826 --> 00:27:47.906
protect their slice of the pie.

00:27:47.906 --> 00:27:48.466
Exactly.

00:27:48.466 --> 00:27:50.386
The higher paid hospitals sued

00:27:50.386 --> 00:27:51.986
arguing the statute requires

00:27:51.986 --> 00:27:53.346
the index to be based on

00:27:53.346 --> 00:27:55.346
actual relative hospital wage

00:27:55.346 --> 00:27:56.146
levels,

00:27:56.146 --> 00:27:58.306
not artificially inflated ones.

00:27:58.306 --> 00:28:00.146
And the D.C. Circuit agreed

00:28:00.146 --> 00:28:01.506
they ruled that CMS

00:28:01.506 --> 00:28:03.346
overstepped its legal bounds.

00:28:03.346 --> 00:28:04.546
And so the policy was struck

00:28:04.546 --> 00:28:04.946
down?

00:28:04.946 --> 00:28:06.546
The policy was struck down.

00:28:06.546 --> 00:28:07.986
The Band-Aid was ripped off.

00:28:07.986 --> 00:28:09.186
And immediately there was

00:28:09.186 --> 00:28:10.066
widespread panic

00:28:10.066 --> 00:28:11.666
in healthcare finance circles.

00:28:11.666 --> 00:28:13.026
I remember reading the headlines

00:28:13.026 --> 00:28:15.666
about a 15.5 percent overnight

00:28:15.666 --> 00:28:16.466
cliff.

00:28:16.466 --> 00:28:17.986
People thought hospital funding

00:28:17.986 --> 00:28:19.266
in Puerto Rico was just going

00:28:19.266 --> 00:28:20.786
to plummet instantly the day

00:28:20.786 --> 00:28:21.586
after the ruling.

00:28:21.586 --> 00:28:22.866
That was the prevailing fear,

00:28:22.866 --> 00:28:23.906
absolutely.

00:28:23.906 --> 00:28:25.426
But we really have to correct

00:28:25.426 --> 00:28:26.466
that misconception

00:28:26.466 --> 00:28:27.746
because the reality is actually

00:28:27.746 --> 00:28:29.106
much more insidious

00:28:29.106 --> 00:28:30.386
than a sudden cliff.

00:28:30.386 --> 00:28:31.186
Okay, explain that.

00:28:31.186 --> 00:28:32.306
It is not a sudden cliff.

00:28:32.306 --> 00:28:34.066
CMS regulations have a built-in

00:28:34.066 --> 00:28:35.186
shock absorber.

00:28:35.186 --> 00:28:36.306
By rule,

00:28:36.306 --> 00:28:37.746
no hospital's wage index can

00:28:37.746 --> 00:28:39.186
fall by more than 5 percent

00:28:39.186 --> 00:28:41.026
in a single year, year over over.

00:28:41.026 --> 00:28:42.626
So instead of dropping straight

00:28:42.626 --> 00:28:44.706
from 0.61 all the way down

00:28:44.706 --> 00:28:46.866
to 0.35 in one day,

00:28:46.866 --> 00:28:47.906
it sort of steps down

00:28:47.906 --> 00:28:48.626
incrementally.

00:28:48.626 --> 00:28:49.266
Correct.

00:28:49.266 --> 00:28:50.786
Puerto Rico is facing a capped

00:28:50.866 --> 00:28:51.826
glide path.

00:28:51.826 --> 00:28:53.666
The wage index drops exactly

00:28:53.666 --> 00:28:55.026
5 percent every single year.

00:28:55.026 --> 00:28:56.466
Okay, let's do the math on that.

00:28:56.466 --> 00:28:57.746
When you run a 5 percent wage

00:28:57.746 --> 00:28:59.506
index cut through that 62 percent

00:28:59.506 --> 00:29:00.866
labor share formula we discussed

00:29:00.866 --> 00:29:01.586
earlier,

00:29:01.586 --> 00:29:03.666
it translates to roughly a 2 percent

00:29:03.666 --> 00:29:04.866
payment cut to the hospital's

00:29:04.866 --> 00:29:05.746
total revenue.

00:29:05.746 --> 00:29:07.346
And that happens every October

00:29:07.346 --> 00:29:09.026
when the new fiscal year begins.

00:29:09.026 --> 00:29:09.426
Yes.

00:29:09.426 --> 00:29:10.466
But because of the distance

00:29:10.466 --> 00:29:12.866
between their boosted 0.61 index

00:29:12.866 --> 00:29:15.506
and their natural 0.35 index

00:29:15.506 --> 00:29:16.866
is so vast,

00:29:16.866 --> 00:29:18.146
they aren't just taking this cut

00:29:18.146 --> 00:29:18.706
once.

00:29:18.706 --> 00:29:19.426
No.

00:29:19.506 --> 00:29:21.186
They will hit that 5 percent cap

00:29:21.186 --> 00:29:22.546
year after year after year.

00:29:22.546 --> 00:29:23.986
This step down process will

00:29:23.986 --> 00:29:25.586
continue for about 8 consecutive

00:29:25.586 --> 00:29:27.026
years until they finally hit the

00:29:27.026 --> 00:29:27.746
absolute bottom.

00:29:27.746 --> 00:29:29.506
Eight years of compounding 2 percent

00:29:29.506 --> 00:29:30.066
cuts.

00:29:30.066 --> 00:29:31.266
If we connect this to the bigger

00:29:31.266 --> 00:29:31.986
picture,

00:29:31.986 --> 00:29:33.426
a slow 2 percent bleed

00:29:33.426 --> 00:29:35.026
is actually far more dangerous

00:29:35.026 --> 00:29:35.906
systemically

00:29:35.906 --> 00:29:37.986
than a sudden 15 percent cliff.

00:29:37.986 --> 00:29:39.266
I think I see we're going with

00:29:39.266 --> 00:29:39.746
this,

00:29:39.746 --> 00:29:41.746
but explain why a smaller cut is

00:29:41.746 --> 00:29:42.466
worse.

00:29:42.466 --> 00:29:43.666
Because a cliff

00:29:43.666 --> 00:29:45.826
creates an immediate undeniable

00:29:45.826 --> 00:29:46.386
crisis.

00:29:46.386 --> 00:29:46.866
Right.

00:29:46.866 --> 00:29:48.786
If revenues drop 15 percent

00:29:48.786 --> 00:29:49.586
overnight,

00:29:49.586 --> 00:29:51.026
it makes the front page of every

00:29:51.026 --> 00:29:51.986
newspaper.

00:29:51.986 --> 00:29:54.386
It causes hospital CEOs to march on

00:29:54.386 --> 00:29:55.266
Washington.

00:29:55.266 --> 00:29:57.106
It forces politicians to draft

00:29:57.106 --> 00:29:58.466
emergency relief bills.

00:29:58.466 --> 00:29:59.026
Right.

00:29:59.026 --> 00:30:01.106
A cliff is acute trauma.

00:30:01.106 --> 00:30:02.546
And systems respond to acute

00:30:02.546 --> 00:30:03.506
trauma,

00:30:03.506 --> 00:30:05.826
but a steady 2 percent bleed

00:30:05.826 --> 00:30:07.186
every single year.

00:30:07.186 --> 00:30:08.706
It avoids the headlines.

00:30:08.706 --> 00:30:09.986
It doesn't trigger emergency

00:30:09.986 --> 00:30:11.346
congressional hearings.

00:30:11.346 --> 00:30:13.026
It just compounds quietly in

00:30:13.026 --> 00:30:13.666
the background.

00:30:13.666 --> 00:30:15.586
The system just slowly suffocates.

00:30:15.586 --> 00:30:16.386
Exactly.

00:30:16.386 --> 00:30:17.506
The hospital doesn't close

00:30:17.506 --> 00:30:17.906
overnight.

00:30:17.906 --> 00:30:19.346
Instead, they just delay upgrading

00:30:19.346 --> 00:30:20.546
the MRI machine.

00:30:20.546 --> 00:30:22.066
They freeze hiring for specialty

00:30:22.066 --> 00:30:22.786
nurses.

00:30:22.786 --> 00:30:24.466
They quietly close a few beds on

00:30:24.466 --> 00:30:25.746
the fourth floor.

00:30:25.746 --> 00:30:27.186
By the time the public realizes

00:30:27.186 --> 00:30:28.386
the system is fundamentally

00:30:28.386 --> 00:30:29.266
collapsing,

00:30:29.266 --> 00:30:30.546
the structural damage is already

00:30:30.546 --> 00:30:30.946
done.

00:30:30.946 --> 00:30:32.226
And the talent has left the

00:30:32.226 --> 00:30:32.626
island.

00:30:32.626 --> 00:30:32.786
Yeah.

00:30:32.786 --> 00:30:34.066
The doctors moved to Florida

00:30:34.066 --> 00:30:34.946
or Texas,

00:30:34.946 --> 00:30:36.706
and it is incredibly difficult

00:30:36.706 --> 00:30:38.146
to rebuild that from scratch.

00:30:38.146 --> 00:30:39.906
It's literally the frog in boiling

00:30:39.906 --> 00:30:41.106
water scenario.

00:30:41.106 --> 00:30:42.226
But just when you think the

00:30:42.226 --> 00:30:43.506
situation for Puerto Rico can't

00:30:43.506 --> 00:30:44.466
get any worse,

00:30:44.466 --> 00:30:46.146
there is a second blade to

00:30:46.146 --> 00:30:47.426
this financial guillotine.

00:30:47.426 --> 00:30:48.306
Yes, there is.

00:30:48.306 --> 00:30:49.986
These wage index cuts don't just

00:30:49.986 --> 00:30:51.986
hit traditional fee-for-service

00:30:51.986 --> 00:30:52.546
Medicare.

00:30:52.546 --> 00:30:54.306
They hit Medicare Advantage.

00:30:54.306 --> 00:30:55.586
And for Puerto Rico,

00:30:55.586 --> 00:30:57.666
that is where the real devastation

00:30:57.666 --> 00:30:58.386
lies.

00:30:58.386 --> 00:30:59.906
This is the critical asymmetry of

00:30:59.906 --> 00:31:01.186
Puerto Rico's healthcare system

00:31:01.186 --> 00:31:02.466
compared to the rest of the nation.

00:31:02.466 --> 00:31:03.906
On the U.S. mainland,

00:31:03.906 --> 00:31:06.466
about 55.5% of Medicare

00:31:06.466 --> 00:31:08.386
beneficiaries choose to enroll

00:31:08.386 --> 00:31:09.426
in Medicare Advantage,

00:31:09.426 --> 00:31:10.946
which are these private plans

00:31:10.946 --> 00:31:12.306
subsidized by the government.

00:31:12.306 --> 00:31:13.506
And the rest use traditional

00:31:13.506 --> 00:31:14.066
Medicare.

00:31:14.066 --> 00:31:14.466
Right.

00:31:14.466 --> 00:31:15.426
But in Puerto Rico,

00:31:15.426 --> 00:31:16.866
it's 96.2%.

00:31:16.946 --> 00:31:18.226
96.2%.

00:31:18.226 --> 00:31:20.146
The vast overwhelming majority of

00:31:20.146 --> 00:31:22.146
seniors rely on these private MA

00:31:22.146 --> 00:31:22.546
plans.

00:31:23.266 --> 00:31:24.866
Traditional Medicare is just a

00:31:24.866 --> 00:31:26.466
tiny fraction of the market there.

00:31:26.466 --> 00:31:28.466
So how does a cut to a hospital's

00:31:28.466 --> 00:31:30.306
traditional Medicare wage index

00:31:30.306 --> 00:31:31.586
actually affect the private

00:31:31.586 --> 00:31:33.186
Medicare Advantage plans?

00:31:33.186 --> 00:31:34.546
Because they are mathematically

00:31:34.546 --> 00:31:35.186
linked.

00:31:35.186 --> 00:31:36.626
Medicare Advantage benchmarks,

00:31:36.626 --> 00:31:37.826
which is the maximum amount of

00:31:37.826 --> 00:31:39.426
money Medicare will pay a private

00:31:39.426 --> 00:31:41.186
plan per enrollee in a specific

00:31:41.186 --> 00:31:42.066
county,

00:31:42.066 --> 00:31:44.226
are calculated using local fee-for-service

00:31:44.226 --> 00:31:45.026
spending data.

00:31:45.026 --> 00:31:45.506
Oh.

00:31:45.506 --> 00:31:47.026
That data is then adjusted by

00:31:47.026 --> 00:31:47.666
something called the

00:31:47.666 --> 00:31:49.346
Average Geographic Adjustment,

00:31:49.346 --> 00:31:50.626
or AGA.

00:31:50.626 --> 00:31:52.386
And the AGA looks at a five-year

00:31:52.386 --> 00:31:53.346
rolling average

00:31:53.346 --> 00:31:55.026
of historical claims data.

00:31:55.026 --> 00:31:55.666
Wait.

00:31:55.666 --> 00:31:57.506
If the AGA looks at a five-year

00:31:57.506 --> 00:31:58.946
historical average,

00:31:58.946 --> 00:32:00.226
wouldn't that dilute the impact

00:32:00.226 --> 00:32:02.466
of a 5% wage index cut?

00:32:02.466 --> 00:32:03.186
Like,

00:32:03.186 --> 00:32:05.346
if I have one bad year of data

00:32:05.346 --> 00:32:07.186
mixed with four good years,

00:32:07.186 --> 00:32:09.026
the average should soften the blow,

00:32:09.026 --> 00:32:09.346
right?

00:32:09.346 --> 00:32:11.266
It would only be one-fifth of the impact.

00:32:11.266 --> 00:32:13.186
That is the highly logical assumption

00:32:13.186 --> 00:32:14.466
that many policymakers

00:32:14.466 --> 00:32:15.506
initially made.

00:32:15.506 --> 00:32:17.106
But I'm guessing it's wrong.

00:32:17.106 --> 00:32:18.546
It is entirely incorrect.

00:32:19.186 --> 00:32:20.626
Because of a specific

00:32:20.626 --> 00:32:22.146
repricing mechanism

00:32:22.146 --> 00:32:23.426
hidden deep in the formula.

00:32:23.426 --> 00:32:24.706
Repricing mechanism.

00:32:24.706 --> 00:32:25.026
Yes.

00:32:25.666 --> 00:32:27.746
When CMS calculates the AGA,

00:32:27.746 --> 00:32:29.026
they don't just average out the

00:32:29.026 --> 00:32:30.386
dollar amounts that were actually

00:32:30.386 --> 00:32:31.586
paid in the past.

00:32:31.586 --> 00:32:32.786
They take those five years of

00:32:32.786 --> 00:32:34.066
historical claims

00:32:34.066 --> 00:32:35.746
and they reprice them as if they

00:32:35.746 --> 00:32:37.266
were being paid under today's

00:32:37.266 --> 00:32:38.626
current wage index rules.

00:32:38.626 --> 00:32:39.346
Oh, wow.

00:32:39.346 --> 00:32:41.506
So they retroactively apply the cut

00:32:41.506 --> 00:32:42.866
to the historical data.

00:32:42.866 --> 00:32:43.586
Exactly.

00:32:43.586 --> 00:32:44.626
They apply the current year's

00:32:44.626 --> 00:32:46.066
depressed wage index cut

00:32:46.066 --> 00:32:47.986
to the entire five-year historical

00:32:47.986 --> 00:32:48.706
base period.

00:32:48.706 --> 00:32:50.306
So the cut doesn't get watered down at all?

00:32:50.306 --> 00:32:50.946
Not at all.

00:32:50.946 --> 00:32:52.146
It hits the Medicare Advantage

00:32:52.146 --> 00:32:53.506
benchmark essentially in full.

00:32:53.506 --> 00:32:54.466
That is brutal.

00:32:54.466 --> 00:32:56.146
It reaches the MA benchmarks

00:32:56.146 --> 00:32:57.346
essentially in full.

00:32:57.346 --> 00:32:58.626
About one calendar year

00:32:58.626 --> 00:33:00.466
after the cut hits the hospitals.

00:33:00.466 --> 00:33:01.746
This is the multiplier effect

00:33:01.746 --> 00:33:02.546
of the tragedy.

00:33:02.546 --> 00:33:04.066
Break that multiplier effect down.

00:33:04.066 --> 00:33:04.626
Sure.

00:33:04.626 --> 00:33:05.826
A wage index cut

00:33:05.826 --> 00:33:07.586
to a traditional Medicare system

00:33:07.586 --> 00:33:10.066
that serves maybe 27,000 people

00:33:10.066 --> 00:33:11.266
in Puerto Rico

00:33:11.266 --> 00:33:12.866
flows directly into a formula

00:33:12.866 --> 00:33:14.146
that dictates the funding

00:33:14.146 --> 00:33:16.066
for over 600,000 seniors

00:33:16.066 --> 00:33:17.346
enrolled in Medicare Advantage.

00:33:17.346 --> 00:33:18.306
Oh, wow.

00:33:18.306 --> 00:33:19.986
It is a massive compounding

00:33:19.986 --> 00:33:21.426
drain of resources

00:33:21.426 --> 00:33:23.186
that affects nearly every single

00:33:23.186 --> 00:33:24.466
elderly person on the island.

00:33:24.466 --> 00:33:24.706
Okay.

00:33:24.706 --> 00:33:26.226
So the picture is incredibly grim.

00:33:26.226 --> 00:33:27.506
The island is bleeding out

00:33:27.506 --> 00:33:28.866
2% a year,

00:33:28.866 --> 00:33:30.146
quietly and relentlessly

00:33:30.146 --> 00:33:31.906
with massive downstream effects

00:33:31.906 --> 00:33:34.066
on hundreds of thousands of seniors.

00:33:34.066 --> 00:33:35.186
Which brings us all the way

00:33:35.186 --> 00:33:36.226
back to our starting point.

00:33:36.226 --> 00:33:36.706
Feeek.

00:33:36.706 --> 00:33:37.666
Right, the new hospital.

00:33:37.666 --> 00:33:38.786
How does an unopened

00:33:38.786 --> 00:33:40.626
$85 million facility

00:33:40.626 --> 00:33:42.466
on an island of 8,000 people

00:33:42.466 --> 00:33:44.146
stop a macroeconomic crisis

00:33:44.146 --> 00:33:45.986
error for 3.2 million people?

00:33:45.986 --> 00:33:46.786
It all comes back

00:33:46.786 --> 00:33:48.146
to the Nantucket precedent.

00:33:48.146 --> 00:33:50.546
The 1997 Rural Floor Statute

00:33:50.546 --> 00:33:52.306
and the literal unapologetic text

00:33:52.306 --> 00:33:53.266
of the law.

00:33:53.266 --> 00:33:55.266
Remember, VX is geographically rural.

00:33:55.266 --> 00:33:56.466
It sits completely outside

00:33:56.466 --> 00:33:57.746
the San Juan Metropolitan

00:33:57.746 --> 00:33:58.706
Statistical Area

00:33:58.706 --> 00:34:00.386
or any other MSA.

00:34:00.386 --> 00:34:03.186
It is rural by pure geography,

00:34:03.186 --> 00:34:04.226
just like Nantucket.

00:34:04.226 --> 00:34:05.266
Exactly.

00:34:05.266 --> 00:34:07.026
The day the new Susana Centino

00:34:07.026 --> 00:34:08.626
facility is officially licensed

00:34:08.626 --> 00:34:09.746
by the Puerto Rican Department

00:34:09.746 --> 00:34:10.386
of Health

00:34:10.386 --> 00:34:11.506
and subsequently Medicare

00:34:11.506 --> 00:34:13.026
certified as a hospital.

00:34:13.026 --> 00:34:14.546
It has to be a specific type

00:34:14.546 --> 00:34:15.346
of hospital, right?

00:34:15.346 --> 00:34:16.066
Yes.

00:34:16.066 --> 00:34:18.146
Specifically, an inpatient

00:34:18.146 --> 00:34:19.586
prospective payment system

00:34:19.586 --> 00:34:22.466
hospital, an IPPS hospital.

00:34:22.466 --> 00:34:23.746
Not just a diagnostic clinic

00:34:23.746 --> 00:34:24.626
or a CDT.

00:34:24.626 --> 00:34:25.426
Okay.

00:34:25.426 --> 00:34:26.226
But the day it gets

00:34:26.226 --> 00:34:28.066
that IPPS certification,

00:34:28.066 --> 00:34:29.186
the machine turns on.

00:34:30.066 --> 00:34:31.186
Its local payroll

00:34:31.186 --> 00:34:32.146
automatically becomes

00:34:32.146 --> 00:34:32.946
the rural floor

00:34:32.946 --> 00:34:34.146
for all of Puerto Rico.

00:34:34.146 --> 00:34:34.786
Automatic.

00:34:34.786 --> 00:34:35.826
Automatically.

00:34:35.826 --> 00:34:37.586
There are no petitions to file.

00:34:37.586 --> 00:34:38.946
No new laws need to be

00:34:38.946 --> 00:34:40.066
debated or passed by

00:34:40.066 --> 00:34:40.786
Congress.

00:34:40.786 --> 00:34:42.946
It is an automatic statutory trigger.

00:34:42.946 --> 00:34:44.466
So the moment they hire staff

00:34:44.466 --> 00:34:45.746
as a certified hospital,

00:34:46.306 --> 00:34:47.906
their specific wages

00:34:47.906 --> 00:34:49.266
set the basement for every

00:34:49.266 --> 00:34:50.706
single urban hospital

00:34:50.706 --> 00:34:52.626
in San Juan, Ponce,

00:34:52.626 --> 00:34:53.986
everywhere else on the main island.

00:34:53.986 --> 00:34:54.466
Yes.

00:34:54.466 --> 00:34:55.746
So what does the math

00:34:55.746 --> 00:34:56.546
actually look like?

00:34:56.546 --> 00:34:57.826
Like how high does the wage

00:34:57.826 --> 00:34:58.626
NVX need to be

00:34:58.626 --> 00:34:59.586
to stop the bleeding?

00:34:59.586 --> 00:35:00.706
The target wage isn't just

00:35:00.706 --> 00:35:01.826
an aspirational number

00:35:01.826 --> 00:35:03.026
picked out of thin air.

00:35:03.026 --> 00:35:04.466
It's pure arithmetic.

00:35:04.466 --> 00:35:04.866
Okay.

00:35:04.866 --> 00:35:06.146
Walk me through the arithmetic.

00:35:06.146 --> 00:35:07.586
To calculate the wage index,

00:35:07.586 --> 00:35:09.266
you take the average hourly wage

00:35:09.266 --> 00:35:10.386
you are paying your staff.

00:35:10.786 --> 00:35:12.306
And you divide it by the national

00:35:12.306 --> 00:35:13.906
average hourly wage divisor.

00:35:13.906 --> 00:35:15.266
And what is that divisor?

00:35:15.266 --> 00:35:17.186
For fiscal year 2027,

00:35:17.186 --> 00:35:18.706
CMS has mathematically set

00:35:18.706 --> 00:35:19.986
that national divisor

00:35:19.986 --> 00:35:21.906
at $60.43.

00:35:21.906 --> 00:35:23.106
So the formula is

00:35:23.106 --> 00:35:26.306
VX wage divided by $60.43

00:35:26.306 --> 00:35:28.226
equals the rural floor index.

00:35:28.226 --> 00:35:28.546
Correct.

00:35:28.546 --> 00:35:29.746
So let's do an example.

00:35:29.746 --> 00:35:31.826
If the operators of the VX hospital

00:35:31.826 --> 00:35:32.706
fund their payroll

00:35:32.706 --> 00:35:33.586
to pay their staff

00:35:33.586 --> 00:35:36.146
an average of $25.40 an hour.

00:35:36.146 --> 00:35:37.026
And you divide that

00:35:37.026 --> 00:35:39.986
by the $60.43 national divisor.

00:35:39.986 --> 00:35:41.346
You create a wage index floor

00:35:41.346 --> 00:35:42.866
of roughly 0.42.

00:35:42.866 --> 00:35:44.066
Okay, 0.42.

00:35:44.066 --> 00:35:45.346
But if they decide to heavily

00:35:45.346 --> 00:35:46.226
invest in their staff,

00:35:46.226 --> 00:35:47.106
say they pay an average

00:35:47.106 --> 00:35:49.266
of $30.25 an hour,

00:35:49.266 --> 00:35:50.066
that creates a floor

00:35:50.066 --> 00:35:51.586
of roughly 0.50.

00:35:51.586 --> 00:35:52.146
And remember,

00:35:52.146 --> 00:35:53.666
the natural unboosted index

00:35:53.666 --> 00:35:54.226
for the island

00:35:54.226 --> 00:35:55.266
is currently stepping down

00:35:55.266 --> 00:35:56.386
toward 0.35.

00:35:56.386 --> 00:35:56.786
Right.

00:35:56.786 --> 00:35:58.626
So a rural floor of 0.42

00:35:58.626 --> 00:35:59.826
or especially 0.50

00:35:59.826 --> 00:36:01.026
would act as this massive

00:36:01.026 --> 00:36:02.386
concrete safety net,

00:36:02.386 --> 00:36:03.666
catching every single urban

00:36:03.666 --> 00:36:04.546
hospital in Puerto Rico

00:36:04.546 --> 00:36:05.666
before they hit the bottom

00:36:05.666 --> 00:36:06.626
exactly the same way

00:36:06.626 --> 00:36:08.066
Nantucket caught Boston.

00:36:08.066 --> 00:36:09.506
It stops the glide path

00:36:09.506 --> 00:36:11.106
dead in its tracks

00:36:11.106 --> 00:36:13.266
and reverses the trend entirely.

00:36:13.266 --> 00:36:15.746
But there is a massive

00:36:15.746 --> 00:36:17.986
incredibly dangerous trap here.

00:36:17.986 --> 00:36:19.826
Sequencing is absolutely everything.

00:36:19.826 --> 00:36:21.186
The imputed floor trap.

00:36:21.186 --> 00:36:22.386
Walk me through this mechanism

00:36:22.386 --> 00:36:23.426
because if the local government

00:36:23.426 --> 00:36:24.946
messes up the sequencing here,

00:36:24.946 --> 00:36:26.786
they don't just fail to help.

00:36:26.786 --> 00:36:28.066
They actually make the financial

00:36:28.066 --> 00:36:29.586
crisis significantly worse.

00:36:30.146 --> 00:36:31.266
Yeah, it could backfire

00:36:31.266 --> 00:36:32.466
catastrophically.

00:36:32.466 --> 00:36:33.826
Currently, because Puerto Rico

00:36:33.826 --> 00:36:35.586
has zero rural hospitals,

00:36:35.586 --> 00:36:37.426
they benefit from a special

00:36:37.426 --> 00:36:38.786
temporary federal provision

00:36:38.786 --> 00:36:41.026
called an imputed statewide floor.

00:36:41.026 --> 00:36:42.146
An imputed floor.

00:36:42.146 --> 00:36:43.106
What does that mean?

00:36:43.106 --> 00:36:45.106
It's essentially a placeholder safety net.

00:36:45.106 --> 00:36:47.106
For 2027, this imputed floor

00:36:47.106 --> 00:36:47.986
provides a safety net

00:36:47.986 --> 00:36:49.826
at an index of 0.3278.

00:36:50.386 --> 00:36:51.026
OK.

00:36:51.026 --> 00:36:52.226
Now it's not a great number,

00:36:52.226 --> 00:36:53.186
but it's far better

00:36:53.186 --> 00:36:55.106
than their absolute raw data

00:36:55.106 --> 00:36:56.066
which would drag them down

00:36:56.066 --> 00:36:57.426
to around 0.25.

00:36:57.426 --> 00:36:58.786
So they currently have a weak

00:36:58.786 --> 00:37:00.146
placeholder safety net

00:37:00.146 --> 00:37:01.986
at 0.3278.

00:37:01.986 --> 00:37:02.466
Right.

00:37:02.466 --> 00:37:03.666
But the danger is this.

00:37:04.386 --> 00:37:05.906
The law explicitly states

00:37:05.906 --> 00:37:06.946
that the moment a real

00:37:06.946 --> 00:37:08.226
rural hospital opens

00:37:08.226 --> 00:37:09.666
and files its first cost report

00:37:09.666 --> 00:37:10.626
with CMS,

00:37:10.626 --> 00:37:12.066
the imputed floor is legally

00:37:12.066 --> 00:37:12.706
extinguished.

00:37:12.706 --> 00:37:14.306
But the placeholder vanishes.

00:37:14.306 --> 00:37:15.906
The real rural data

00:37:15.906 --> 00:37:17.746
instantly replaces the placeholder.

00:37:17.746 --> 00:37:19.106
So imagine the Vicks Hospital

00:37:19.106 --> 00:37:20.466
opens tomorrow.

00:37:20.466 --> 00:37:21.506
But instead of funding

00:37:21.506 --> 00:37:22.386
a high payroll

00:37:22.386 --> 00:37:23.826
to create a high floor,

00:37:23.826 --> 00:37:24.946
they just pay standard

00:37:24.946 --> 00:37:26.146
depressed Puerto Rican wages

00:37:26.146 --> 00:37:27.586
say $15 an hour.

00:37:27.586 --> 00:37:28.466
OK, let's do the math

00:37:28.466 --> 00:37:29.666
on $15 an hour.

00:37:29.666 --> 00:37:30.866
If you divide $15

00:37:30.866 --> 00:37:33.026
by the $60 and 43s divisor,

00:37:33.026 --> 00:37:34.466
that translates to a real

00:37:34.466 --> 00:37:36.306
rural floor of about 0.25

00:37:36.306 --> 00:37:37.106
level one.

00:37:37.106 --> 00:37:37.746
Wait.

00:37:37.746 --> 00:37:39.106
So if Vicks opens

00:37:39.106 --> 00:37:40.386
paying minimum wage,

00:37:40.386 --> 00:37:41.426
they would accidentally

00:37:41.426 --> 00:37:43.826
overwrite the 0.3278

00:37:43.826 --> 00:37:44.866
imputed safety net

00:37:44.866 --> 00:37:45.346
Yes.

00:37:45.346 --> 00:37:46.066
And replace it

00:37:46.066 --> 00:37:48.546
with a 0.2511 floor.

00:37:48.546 --> 00:37:49.666
They would actively

00:37:49.666 --> 00:37:50.466
lower the floor

00:37:50.466 --> 00:37:51.666
for the entire territory

00:37:51.666 --> 00:37:53.346
costing the islands hospitals

00:37:53.346 --> 00:37:54.466
millions of dollars.

00:37:54.466 --> 00:37:55.266
Exactly.

00:37:55.266 --> 00:37:56.626
A well-meaning rural hospital

00:37:56.626 --> 00:37:57.666
if it's opened carelessly

00:37:57.666 --> 00:37:58.706
without properly funded

00:37:58.706 --> 00:37:59.746
and planned payrolls

00:37:59.746 --> 00:38:01.026
turns the lever backward.

00:38:01.026 --> 00:38:01.906
It becomes a weapon

00:38:01.906 --> 00:38:02.546
against the system

00:38:02.546 --> 00:38:03.346
rather than a shield.

00:38:03.346 --> 00:38:03.906
So they have to get

00:38:03.906 --> 00:38:04.946
the money in place first.

00:38:04.946 --> 00:38:06.546
The wage level in Vicks

00:38:06.546 --> 00:38:07.666
must be fully funded

00:38:07.666 --> 00:38:09.506
committed to and executing

00:38:09.506 --> 00:38:10.386
before it files

00:38:10.386 --> 00:38:12.146
its first Medicare cost report.

00:38:12.146 --> 00:38:13.426
Timing is everything.

00:38:13.426 --> 00:38:14.466
Now, some listeners

00:38:14.466 --> 00:38:15.506
who know healthcare finance

00:38:15.506 --> 00:38:16.386
might be listening to this

00:38:16.386 --> 00:38:17.186
and thinking,

00:38:17.186 --> 00:38:18.306
wait, aren't there other ways

00:38:18.306 --> 00:38:18.866
to do this?

00:38:19.426 --> 00:38:20.306
What about just taking

00:38:20.306 --> 00:38:21.826
a huge existing hospital

00:38:21.826 --> 00:38:22.786
in San Juan

00:38:22.786 --> 00:38:23.826
and having them legally

00:38:23.826 --> 00:38:25.746
reclassify as rural?

00:38:25.746 --> 00:38:26.706
That is often referred

00:38:26.706 --> 00:38:28.146
to as path B.

00:38:28.146 --> 00:38:29.426
It's the urban to rural

00:38:29.426 --> 00:38:30.546
reclassification

00:38:30.546 --> 00:38:32.786
under section 412.103

00:38:32.786 --> 00:38:34.146
of the regulations.

00:38:34.146 --> 00:38:35.586
And legally,

00:38:35.586 --> 00:38:37.346
yes, a massive hospital

00:38:37.346 --> 00:38:39.586
with a high payroll in San Juan

00:38:39.586 --> 00:38:41.906
could theoretically petition CMS

00:38:41.906 --> 00:38:43.426
to be classified as rural

00:38:43.426 --> 00:38:44.786
in order to set the floor.

00:38:44.786 --> 00:38:46.066
So why not just do that?

00:38:46.066 --> 00:38:47.426
Why go through all the trouble

00:38:47.426 --> 00:38:49.426
to rely on a brand new

00:38:49.426 --> 00:38:50.466
untested facility

00:38:50.466 --> 00:38:51.506
on a tiny island?

00:38:52.226 --> 00:38:52.946
It really comes down

00:38:52.946 --> 00:38:54.546
to a classic economic dilemma.

00:38:55.106 --> 00:38:56.146
The free rider problem.

00:38:56.146 --> 00:38:57.186
Let's use an analogy.

00:38:57.186 --> 00:38:57.506
Okay.

00:38:57.506 --> 00:38:58.706
Imagine a massive apartment

00:38:58.706 --> 00:38:59.026
building where

00:38:59.026 --> 00:39:00.626
the electrical grid is failing.

00:39:00.626 --> 00:39:01.506
One tenant decides

00:39:01.506 --> 00:39:02.626
to pay out of their own pocket

00:39:02.626 --> 00:39:03.586
to completely upgrade

00:39:03.586 --> 00:39:04.546
the entire building's

00:39:04.546 --> 00:39:05.666
electrical system.

00:39:05.666 --> 00:39:06.866
They bear the massive cost

00:39:06.866 --> 00:39:07.506
themselves.

00:39:07.506 --> 00:39:07.906
Right.

00:39:07.906 --> 00:39:09.666
But once the grid is fixed,

00:39:09.666 --> 00:39:10.706
all the other landlords

00:39:10.706 --> 00:39:11.426
in the building

00:39:11.426 --> 00:39:12.386
get to instantly charge

00:39:12.386 --> 00:39:13.026
higher rent

00:39:13.026 --> 00:39:14.466
because the building is nicer

00:39:14.466 --> 00:39:15.826
without having spent a dime.

00:39:15.826 --> 00:39:16.706
The other landlords

00:39:16.706 --> 00:39:17.826
are the free riders.

00:39:17.826 --> 00:39:18.626
Right.

00:39:18.626 --> 00:39:19.986
In the hospital scenario,

00:39:19.986 --> 00:39:21.106
if a massive hospital

00:39:21.106 --> 00:39:22.546
in San Juan decides to

00:39:23.106 --> 00:39:24.706
artificially inflate its wages

00:39:24.706 --> 00:39:26.866
to say $30 an hour

00:39:26.866 --> 00:39:28.226
just to set the rural floor,

00:39:29.266 --> 00:39:31.106
that specific hospital

00:39:31.106 --> 00:39:32.866
has to bear the multimillion

00:39:32.866 --> 00:39:33.826
dollar burden

00:39:33.826 --> 00:39:35.426
of that inflated payroll

00:39:35.426 --> 00:39:36.466
entirely on its own

00:39:36.466 --> 00:39:37.506
balance sheet.

00:39:37.506 --> 00:39:39.506
But the benefit of that action,

00:39:39.506 --> 00:39:40.786
the higher Medicare payments

00:39:40.786 --> 00:39:42.066
generated by the new

00:39:42.066 --> 00:39:43.906
.50 floor flows

00:39:43.906 --> 00:39:45.026
to every other competing

00:39:45.026 --> 00:39:46.306
hospital on the island.

00:39:46.306 --> 00:39:47.346
You're basically asking

00:39:47.346 --> 00:39:48.466
a hospital CFO

00:39:48.466 --> 00:39:49.906
to double their own payroll

00:39:49.906 --> 00:39:51.026
costs out of the goodness

00:39:51.026 --> 00:39:51.986
of their heart,

00:39:51.986 --> 00:39:52.866
deliberately hurting

00:39:52.866 --> 00:39:54.386
their own operating margins

00:39:54.386 --> 00:39:55.186
so that their direct

00:39:55.186 --> 00:39:56.466
competitors across the street

00:39:56.466 --> 00:39:57.826
can get a massive revenue

00:39:57.826 --> 00:39:58.706
boost from Medicare.

00:39:58.706 --> 00:39:59.346
Exactly.

00:39:59.346 --> 00:40:00.466
And no hospital board

00:40:00.466 --> 00:40:01.586
of directors is going to

00:40:01.586 --> 00:40:02.386
sustain that kind

00:40:02.386 --> 00:40:03.826
of corporate altruism.

00:40:03.826 --> 00:40:04.706
They have a fiduciary

00:40:04.706 --> 00:40:05.906
duty to their own survival.

00:40:05.906 --> 00:40:06.546
Makes sense.

00:40:06.546 --> 00:40:08.706
Nantucket pays $78 an hour

00:40:08.706 --> 00:40:10.066
because its own isolated

00:40:10.066 --> 00:40:11.666
island economy demands it.

00:40:11.666 --> 00:40:12.626
They aren't doing it to do

00:40:12.626 --> 00:40:13.426
Boston a favor.

00:40:14.066 --> 00:40:15.346
VAX is the exact same.

00:40:15.346 --> 00:40:16.786
It has no free riders.

00:40:16.786 --> 00:40:17.906
It is one hospital,

00:40:17.906 --> 00:40:19.106
one localized payroll,

00:40:19.106 --> 00:40:20.306
one decision maker.

00:40:20.306 --> 00:40:21.346
So we're talking about

00:40:21.346 --> 00:40:22.946
a massive systemic manipulation

00:40:22.946 --> 00:40:24.546
based purely on geography.

00:40:25.186 --> 00:40:25.906
I have to ask

00:40:25.906 --> 00:40:27.186
the care versus compliance

00:40:27.186 --> 00:40:27.906
question here.

00:40:27.906 --> 00:40:28.466
Yeah.

00:40:28.466 --> 00:40:29.506
Is this just financial

00:40:29.506 --> 00:40:30.066
engineering?

00:40:30.066 --> 00:40:31.026
Like are we just gaming

00:40:31.026 --> 00:40:32.146
a spreadsheet here,

00:40:32.146 --> 00:40:33.266
finding the Puerto Rican

00:40:33.266 --> 00:40:34.626
equivalent of the Nantucket

00:40:34.626 --> 00:40:35.346
loophole?

00:40:35.346 --> 00:40:36.946
What's fascinating here

00:40:36.946 --> 00:40:38.786
is the moral center

00:40:38.786 --> 00:40:40.866
of this specific strategy.

00:40:40.866 --> 00:40:41.906
If you reclassify

00:40:41.906 --> 00:40:43.986
an urban hospital in San Juan,

00:40:43.986 --> 00:40:45.586
just to manipulate the numbers,

00:40:45.586 --> 00:40:47.026
yes, that is pure spreadsheet

00:40:47.026 --> 00:40:47.826
manipulation.

00:40:47.826 --> 00:40:48.226
Right.

00:40:48.226 --> 00:40:49.826
But the VAX strategy

00:40:49.826 --> 00:40:51.506
is fundamentally different.

00:40:51.506 --> 00:40:53.106
This strategy ends

00:40:53.106 --> 00:40:55.186
with a genuinely rural,

00:40:55.186 --> 00:40:57.586
geographically isolated island,

00:40:57.586 --> 00:40:59.026
an island where a 13-year-old

00:40:59.026 --> 00:41:00.866
girl died in agonizing death

00:41:00.866 --> 00:41:01.586
because they didn't have a

00:41:01.586 --> 00:41:03.026
basic emergency room,

00:41:03.026 --> 00:41:04.626
finally getting a fully staffed,

00:41:04.626 --> 00:41:06.306
well-paid, highly competent

00:41:06.306 --> 00:41:07.186
medical team.

00:41:07.186 --> 00:41:08.226
The policy lever

00:41:08.226 --> 00:41:09.026
and the human need

00:41:09.026 --> 00:41:10.866
are the exact same object.

00:41:10.866 --> 00:41:12.066
By funding the doctors,

00:41:12.066 --> 00:41:13.186
nurses, and technicians

00:41:13.186 --> 00:41:14.306
in VAX at a high level

00:41:14.306 --> 00:41:15.666
to save lives locally,

00:41:15.666 --> 00:41:16.946
you automatically trigger

00:41:16.946 --> 00:41:17.906
the federal formula

00:41:17.906 --> 00:41:18.786
that saves the healthcare

00:41:18.786 --> 00:41:20.066
system territorially.

00:41:20.066 --> 00:41:21.826
It aligns regulatory compliance

00:41:21.826 --> 00:41:23.746
perfectly with urgent patient care.

00:41:24.386 --> 00:41:25.666
It is using the system's

00:41:25.666 --> 00:41:27.106
own rules to correct

00:41:27.106 --> 00:41:29.186
a massive historical inequity.

00:41:29.186 --> 00:41:31.106
So the strategy is perfectly legal,

00:41:31.106 --> 00:41:32.706
the math is totally sound,

00:41:32.706 --> 00:41:33.906
and the moral imperative

00:41:33.906 --> 00:41:35.106
is extremely clear.

00:41:35.106 --> 00:41:35.666
Right.

00:41:35.666 --> 00:41:36.546
But we need to look

00:41:36.546 --> 00:41:37.666
at where things stand

00:41:37.666 --> 00:41:39.106
right now in the real world.

00:41:39.986 --> 00:41:40.946
Let's fast forward

00:41:40.946 --> 00:41:42.066
to our current status.

00:41:42.786 --> 00:41:45.106
It is September 1st, 2026.

00:41:45.746 --> 00:41:47.186
The building is physically

00:41:47.186 --> 00:41:48.466
sitting there on the island.

00:41:49.346 --> 00:41:50.386
What is the status

00:41:50.386 --> 00:41:52.866
of this $85 million facility?

00:41:52.866 --> 00:41:54.066
Well, the physical infrastructure

00:41:54.066 --> 00:41:55.586
is incredibly impressive.

00:41:55.586 --> 00:41:56.626
The building was officially

00:41:56.626 --> 00:41:57.586
delivered to the Puerto Rico

00:41:57.586 --> 00:41:58.386
Department of Health

00:41:58.386 --> 00:41:59.826
in December of 2025.

00:41:59.826 --> 00:42:00.946
And who paid for it?

00:42:00.946 --> 00:42:03.106
It's an $85 million investment

00:42:03.186 --> 00:42:04.866
funded largely by FEMA,

00:42:04.866 --> 00:42:06.626
along with local government funds.

00:42:06.626 --> 00:42:07.346
And they didn't just

00:42:07.346 --> 00:42:08.706
build a standard clinic,

00:42:08.706 --> 00:42:10.946
they built it for deep resilience.

00:42:10.946 --> 00:42:12.306
It is green certified

00:42:12.306 --> 00:42:14.626
under very strict resilience principles.

00:42:14.626 --> 00:42:16.226
Yeah, I read the specs on this place

00:42:16.226 --> 00:42:17.986
and it is basically a fortress.

00:42:17.986 --> 00:42:21.026
It has 21 days of resilience autonomy.

00:42:21.026 --> 00:42:22.306
Which means it can run on its own

00:42:22.306 --> 00:42:23.026
for three weeks?

00:42:23.026 --> 00:42:23.586
Exactly.

00:42:23.586 --> 00:42:24.946
It has two massive

00:42:24.946 --> 00:42:26.066
redundant generators.

00:42:26.066 --> 00:42:27.186
It has redundant chillers

00:42:27.186 --> 00:42:28.626
for the air conditioning.

00:42:28.626 --> 00:42:30.066
And water tower is holding

00:42:30.066 --> 00:42:31.266
70,000 gallons

00:42:31.266 --> 00:42:32.466
of potable water.

00:42:32.546 --> 00:42:34.706
It is explicitly built to survive

00:42:34.706 --> 00:42:36.306
